LBJ was the ultimate insider who started at the bottom and mastered every rung.

Staff & early appointed: Legislative secretary to a Texas Congressman (1931–1935, networking aggressively as “speaker” of the aides’ “Little Congress”); Texas director of the National Youth Administration (appointed 1935–1937, delivering New Deal jobs and infrastructure).
Elected ladder: U.S. House (elected 1937–1949); U.S. Senate (elected 1949–1961).
Party structures & leadership: Senate Majority Leader (1955–1961), where he wielded unmatched procedural skill and personal persuasion to pass landmark legislation.
Appointed/higher elected: Vice President (1961–1963 under Kennedy); ascended to President (1963–1969).
Johnson’s early staff work and appointed administrative role gave him deep knowledge of Washington and Texas politics; he then climbed Congress while dominating party machinery, using both to reach the White House.

